%% chapter-18.ltx — Scoping Rules and Their Semantics
%% Part II: Programming Language Design

%% ============================================================
%% 18.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:scoping-intro}

Scoping rules determine the visibility and lifetime of bindings in a
program.  They answer the fundamental question: when a name is used, which
declaration does it refer to?  The choice of scoping rules has profound
implications for program structure, modularity, reasoning, and
implementation.

This chapter provides a comprehensive treatment of scoping.  We begin with
lexical (static) scoping, the dominant paradigm in modern languages.  We
then examine dynamic scoping, its uses and limitations.  We study block
structure and nested scopes, the mechanism of closures, hygienic macro
expansion, and the formal semantics of scoping.

\begin{definition}[Scope Rule]\label{def:scope-rule}
  A \emph{scope rule} is a function that maps each name occurrence in a
  program to its binding declaration:
  \[
    \texttt{resolve} : \texttt{Occurrence} \to \texttt{Declaration} \cup \{\texttt{unbound}\}
  \]
\end{definition}

%% ============================================================
%% 18.2 Lexical (Static) Scoping
%% ============================================================
\section{Lexical (Static) Scoping}\label{sec:lexical-scoping}

\subsection{The Principle of Lexical Scoping}\label{sec:lexical-principle}

\begin{definition}[Lexical Scoping]\label{def:lexical-scoping}
  Under \emph{lexical scoping} (also called \emph{static scoping}), the
  scope of a declaration is determined by the program text.  A name
  occurrence refers to the declaration in the nearest enclosing lexical
  scope that contains both the declaration and the occurrence.
\end{definition}

Lexical scoping has a crucial property: \emph{alpha-equivalence}.  Two
programs that differ only in the names of bound variables have the same
meaning:
\[
  \lambda x.\, x + 1 \equiv_\alpha \lambda y.\, y + 1
\]

\begin{historical}
Lexical scoping was introduced by the Lambda Calculus (Church, 1932) and
first implemented in a programming language by ALGOL~60 (for static
variables).  It was popularized by Scheme (1975) and is now the standard in
most programming languages.
\end{historical}

\subsection{Lexical Scoping in Practice}\label{sec:lexical-practice}

\begin{example}[Lexical Scoping in Multiple Languages]\label{ex:lexical-scoping}
  \textbf{Python}:
  \begin{verbatim}
  x = 10
  def f():
      print(x)  # refers to global x = 10
  def g():
      x = 20
      f()       # still prints 10 (lexical scoping)
  \end{verbatim}
  
  \textbf{JavaScript}:
  \begin{verbatim}
  let x = 10;
  function f() { console.log(x); }  // refers to outer x = 10
  function g() {
      let x = 20;
      f();  // still prints 10 (lexical scoping)
  }
  \end{verbatim}
  
  \textbf{Rust}:
  \begin{verbatim}
  let x = 10;
  let f = || println!("{}", x);  // captures x = 10
  let g = || {
      let x = 20;
      f();  // still prints 10 (lexical scoping)
  };
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Shadowing}\label{sec:shadowing}

\begin{definition}[Shadowing]\label{def:shadowing}
  A declaration $d_2$ \emph{shadows} a declaration $d_1$ if both are in
  scope at some program point, $d_2$ is in an inner scope, and both
  declare the same name.  At the shadowed point, references to the name
  resolve to $d_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Shadowing]\label{ex:shadowing}
  \begin{verbatim}
  let x = 1 in      (* outer x *)
  let x = 2 in      (* inner x shadows outer x *)
  x                  (* refers to inner x = 2 *)
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

Shadowing is a feature, not a bug: it allows local names to be chosen
without concern for names in enclosing scopes.  But excessive shadowing can
reduce readability.

\subsection{Scoping Rules for Different Constructs}\label{sec:scoping-constructs}

Different language constructs have different scoping behaviors:

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Scoping Rules by Construct}
  \label{tab:scoping-constructs}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Construct} & \textbf{Scope of declared name} \\
    \hline
    \texttt{let} binding & In the body of the \texttt{let} \\
    Function parameter & In the function body \\
    \texttt{for} loop variable & In the loop body (C99, Java, Rust) \\
    Class member & In the class body \\
    Import & From the import point to end of scope \\
    Exception handler parameter & In the handler body \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% 18.3 Dynamic Scoping
%% ============================================================
\section{Dynamic Scoping}\label{sec:dynamic-scoping}

\subsection{The Principle of Dynamic Scoping}\label{sec:dynamic-principle}

\begin{definition}[Dynamic Scoping]\label{def:dynamic-scoping-detail}
  Under \emph{dynamic scoping}, the binding of a name is determined by the
  runtime call stack.  A name occurrence refers to the most recent binding
  established by any caller in the dynamic call chain.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Dynamic Scoping]\label{ex:dynamic-scoping}
  \begin{verbatim}
  let x = 1
  let f = fun () -> x       (* x is dynamically scoped *)
  let g = fun () ->
    let x = 2 in
    f ()                     (* returns 2 under dynamic scoping *)
  \end{verbatim}
  Under dynamic scoping, when \texttt{g} calls \texttt{f}, the binding
  \texttt{x = 2} from \texttt{g} is visible in \texttt{f}.
\end{example}

\subsection{Advantages of Dynamic Scoping}\label{sec:dynamic-advantages}

Dynamic scoping has some legitimate uses:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Configuration}: Global parameters (e.g., output format,
    debug level) that can be temporarily overridden.
  \item \textbf{Exception handling}: The handler is found by searching the
    call stack---a form of dynamic scoping.
  \item \textbf{Special variables}: Emacs Lisp's \texttt{defvar} and Common
    Lisp's \texttt{special} declarations provide controlled dynamic scoping.
  \item \textbf{Implicit parameters}: Haskell's implicit parameters use a
    form of dynamic scoping.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Disadvantages of Dynamic Scoping}\label{sec:dynamic-disadvantages}

Dynamic scoping has serious drawbacks:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Unpredictability}: A function's behavior depends on its
    callers, making it hard to reason about in isolation.
  \item \textbf{Fragility}: Renaming a variable in one function can change
    the behavior of another function.
  \item \textbf{No closures}: Functions cannot capture their environment;
    higher-order functions are less useful.
  \item \textbf{Implementation cost}: The binding must be searched at
    runtime, which is slower than static lookup.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{insight}
Dynamic scoping violates the principle of \emph{modular reasoning}: you
cannot understand a function without knowing all the contexts in which it
might be called.  This is why lexical scoping has become the standard.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Emulating Dynamic Scoping}\label{sec:emulating-dynamic}

In lexically scoped languages, dynamic scoping can be emulated:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Global variables with save/restore}: Save the old value,
    set the new value, execute, restore the old value.
  \item \textbf{Thread-local storage}: Each thread has its own copy of the
    variable.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic-wind}: Scheme's \texttt{dynamic-wind} provides
    guaranteed cleanup when dynamically scoped state is modified.
  \item \textbf{Parameter passing}: Explicitly pass the ``dynamic''
    variable as a parameter (the most reliable approach).
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 18.4 Block Structure and Nested Scopes
%% ============================================================
\section{Block Structure and Nested Scopes}\label{sec:block-structure}

\subsection{Block Structure}\label{sec:block-structure-detail}

\begin{definition}[Block Structure]\label{def:block-structure}
  \emph{Block structure} is the organization of a program into nested
  blocks, each with its own local declarations.  A block is a sequence of
  declarations followed by a sequence of statements.  The scope of each
  declaration extends to the end of the block.
\end{definition}

Block structure was introduced by ALGOL~60 and is now universal:

\begin{example}[Block Structure in C]\label{ex:block-c}
  \begin{verbatim}
  void f() {
      int x = 1;          // scope: entire function
      {
          int y = 2;      // scope: inner block
          int x = 3;      // shadows outer x
          printf("%d", x + y);  // prints 5
      }
      printf("%d", x);    // prints 1
  }
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Nested Functions}\label{sec:nested-functions}

Some languages allow functions to be defined inside other functions:

\begin{example}[Nested Functions in Pascal]\label{ex:nested-pascal}
  \begin{verbatim}
  procedure outer;
    var x: integer;
    procedure inner;
    begin
      x := x + 1;  // refers to outer's x
    end;
  begin
    x := 0;
    inner;
    writeln(x);  // prints 1
  end;
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

Nested functions can access variables from enclosing scopes.  This requires
the implementation to provide a mechanism for accessing non-local variables
(\Cref{sec:closures-impl}).

\subsection{Scope Rules for Recursion}\label{sec:scope-recursion}

Recursive definitions require special scoping rules:

\begin{definition}[Recursive Scope]\label{def:recursive-scope}
  In a \emph{recursive scope}, a declaration is visible within its own
  defining expression.  This enables recursive functions and mutually
  recursive definitions.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Recursive Scoping]\label{ex:recursive-scope}
  \begin{verbatim}
  let rec factorial = fun n ->
    if n <= 1 then 1
    else n * factorial (n - 1)
  \end{verbatim}
  The name \texttt{factorial} is visible within its own definition,
  enabling the recursive call.
\end{example}

