%% 18.5 Closures and Their Implementation
%% ============================================================
\section{Closures and Their Implementation}\label{sec:closures-impl}

\subsection{What Is a Closure?}\label{sec:what-is-closure}

\begin{definition}[Closure]\label{def:closure}
  A \emph{closure} is a function value that captures the lexical
  environment in which it was created.  It consists of:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item A code pointer (the function's implementation).
    \item An environment (the captured bindings of free variables).
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Closure Example]\label{ex:closure}
  \begin{verbatim}
  let make_counter = fun () ->
    let count = ref 0 in
    fun () ->
      count := !count + 1;
      !count
  
  let c1 = make_counter ()
  let c2 = make_counter ()
  
  c1 ()  (* returns 1 *)
  c1 ()  (* returns 2 *)
  c2 ()  (* returns 1 (independent of c1) *)
  \end{verbatim}
  Each call to \texttt{make\_counter} creates a new closure with its own
  \texttt{count} variable.
\end{example}

\subsection{Free Variables and Closed Environments}\label{sec:free-variables}

\begin{definition}[Free Variable]\label{def:free-var}
  A variable $x$ is \emph{free} in an expression $e$ if $x$ occurs in $e$
  but is not bound by any enclosing $\lambda$-abstraction or let-binding in
  $e$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Closed Expression]\label{def:closed-expr}
  An expression is \emph{closed} if it has no free variables.  A closure
  makes a function ``closed'' by capturing its free variables.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Implementation Strategies}\label{sec:closure-implementation}

\subsubsection{Display (Static Link)}

The display technique uses a static link (also called an access link) to
connect each activation record to the activation record of its lexical
parent:

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style={draw, minimum width=2cm, minimum height=0.8cm, align=center},
    >=stealth
  ]
    % Main function
    \node[box] (main) at (0, 0) {\texttt{main}\\$x = 1$};
    \node[box, draw=red] (f) at (0, -1.5) {\texttt{f}\\$y = 2$};
    \node[box, draw=blue] (g) at (0, -3) {\texttt{g}\\$z = 3$};
    
    % Static links
    \draw[->, dashed, red] (f.north) -- node[right] {static link} (main.south);
    \draw[->, dashed, blue] (g.north) -- node[right] {static link} (f.south);
    
    % Dynamic links
    \draw[->, thick] (f.west) -- ++(-1, 0) |- node[left] {dyn} (main.west);
    \draw[->, thick] (g.west) -- ++(-1.5, 0) |- node[left] {dyn} (f.west);
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Static links (lexical) vs.\ dynamic links in the call stack.}
  \label{fig:static-links}
\end{figure}

When a function accesses a non-local variable, it follows the static links
to find the appropriate activation record.  The number of links to follow
is determined at compile time (the \emph{static nesting depth}).

\subsubsection{Closure Records (Heap-Allocated Environments)}

When functions can outlive their creating scope (e.g., returned from
functions, stored in data structures), the environment must be
heap-allocated:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{On function creation:}
\State Allocate an environment record on the heap
\State Copy the values of free variables into the record
\State Return a closure = (code pointer, environment pointer)
\Statex
\State \textbf{On function call:}
\State Load the environment pointer from the closure
\State Access free variables through the environment pointer
\end{algorithmic}

\subsubsection{Lambda Lifting}\label{sec:lambda-lifting}

Lambda lifting eliminates closures by converting free variables into
explicit parameters:

\begin{example}[Lambda Lifting]\label{ex:lambda-lifting}
  Before:
  \begin{verbatim}
  let add = fun x -> fun y -> x + y
  \end{verbatim}
  After lambda lifting:
  \begin{verbatim}
  let add_inner = fun x y -> x + y
  let add = fun x -> fun y -> add_inner x y
  \end{verbatim}
  The free variable \texttt{x} in the inner function becomes an explicit
  parameter.
\end{example}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Sharing Captured Mutable Bindings across Closures}

Copying free-variable values into a flat closure record works for
immutable bindings. For a captured mutable variable, two closures
from the same activation must instead share its storage location.
Assume lexical resolution and capture analysis have assigned
unique binding identifiers, marked mutability, and computed capture
lists. Each activation creates fresh storage for its mutable captured
bindings. Its environment $\rho$ maps mutable captured identifiers
to cell addresses and immutable identifiers to values. The closure
environment uses the same representation. The allocation below is
performed once per binding instance, when its initializer has
already been evaluated.

\begin{sudocode}[Box a binding and build a flat capture record]
\Procedure{BindCaptured}{$\rho,b,v,H$}
  \If{$b$ is mutable}
    \State $a\gets$ fresh address; $H[a]\gets v$
    \State $\rho[b]\gets a$ \Comment{One box per activation}
  \Else
    \State $\rho[b]\gets v$
  \EndIf
\EndProcedure
\Function{Close}{$f,\rho$}
  \State $E\gets$ empty map
  \ForAll{$b$ in the capture list of $f$}
    \State $E[b]\gets\rho[b]$ \Comment{Copy address, not contents}
  \EndFor
  \State \Return closure $(f,E)$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
A factory initializes mutable \texttt{x} to $10$ and returns
\texttt{inc}, which adds one to \texttt{x}, and \texttt{get},
which reads it. The activation binds the identifier of \texttt{x}
to address $42$, with $H[42]=10$. Both calls to \textsc{Close}
store $42$ in their records. Invoking \texttt{inc} follows that
address to write $11$; invoking \texttt{get} follows the same
address to read $11$. A second factory activation can use address
$43$ and still return $10$ from its own \texttt{get}. Binding
identifiers resolve scope, but they do not identify instances
across activations.

\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
All accesses compiled for one mutable binding instance use its one
address: reads load $H[a]$ and assignments store $H[a]$.
Allocating a separate box while building each closure would break
this invariant even though the addresses initially contained equal
values. Closure creation takes one copy per captured binding and
retains the boxes after the factory returns. This sketch assumes
ordinary initialization and sequential mutation; recursive bindings
and cross-thread access require additional rules.

\subsection{Closures in Different Languages}\label{sec:closures-languages}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Closure Support in Languages}
  \label{tab:closures}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Language} & \textbf{Closures} & \textbf{Implementation} \\
    \hline
    ML, OCaml, Haskell & Full & Heap-allocated environments \\
    Scheme & Full & Heap or escape analysis \\
    JavaScript & Full & Heap-allocated \\
    Python & Full (with \texttt{nonlocal}) & Cell objects \\
    Java & Limited (effectively final) & Synthetic fields \\
    C++ & Full (since C++11) & Stack or heap \\
    Rust & Full (with lifetime checking) & Stack or heap \\
    C & No & Manual (function pointer + context) \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

