%% 18.6 Hygienic Macro Expansion
%% ============================================================
\section{Hygienic Macro Expansion}\label{sec:hygienic-macros}

\subsection{The Problem with Macros}\label{sec:macro-problem}

Preprocessor macros (as in C) are notorious for scoping problems:

\begin{example}[Macro Scoping Problem]\label{ex:macro-problem}
  \begin{verbatim}
  #define SWAP(a, b) { int tmp = a; a = b; b = tmp; }
  
  int tmp = 42;
  SWAP(x, y);  // uses the macro's tmp, not the outer tmp
               // but what if the macro used a name from the caller?
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Hygiene}\label{sec:hygiene}

\begin{definition}[Hygiene]\label{def:hygiene}
  A macro system is \emph{hygienic} if macro expansion does not
  inadvertently capture or shadow identifiers.  Specifically:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Names introduced by the macro do not capture free variables in
      the macro body.
    \item Names in the macro arguments are not captured by bindings
      introduced by the macro.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Hygienic Macro in Scheme]\label{ex:hygienic-scheme}
  \begin{verbatim}
  (define-syntax swap!
    (syntax-rules ()
      ((swap! a b)
       (let ((tmp a))
         (set! a b)
         (set! b tmp)))))
  
  (let ((tmp 42))
    (swap! x y))  ; tmp in the macro is different from the outer tmp
  \end{verbatim}
  The hygienic macro system ensures that the macro's \texttt{tmp} does not
  interfere with the caller's \texttt{tmp}.
\end{example}

\subsection{Implementing Hygiene}\label{sec:hygiene-impl}

Hygiene is typically implemented by tagging each identifier with its
\emph{binding context}---a unique identifier for the scope in which it was
written:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{On macro expansion:}
\For{each identifier in the macro template}
  \State Tag it with the macro's scope
\EndFor
\For{each identifier in the macro arguments}
  \State Keep its original scope tag
\EndFor
\State \textbf{On name resolution:}
\State Two identifiers are the same only if they have the same name
  \emph{and} the same scope tag
\end{algorithmic}

\subsection{Rust's Macro System}\label{sec:rust-macros}

Rust provides two macro systems:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{macro\_rules!}: Declarative macros, hygienic by default.
    Identifiers introduced by the macro are in a different scope from the
    caller's identifiers.
  \item \textbf{Procedural macros}: More powerful, but the programmer must
    manage hygiene explicitly.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Rust Hygienic Macro]\label{ex:rust-hygiene}
  \begin{verbatim}
  macro_rules! create_function {
      () => {
          fn helper() {
              println!("I'm a helper!");
          }
      };
  }
  
  create_function!();
  // helper() is accessible, but its internal names don't
  // interfere with the caller's names
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 18.7 Formal Semantics of Scoping
%% ============================================================
\section{Formal Semantics of Scoping}\label{sec:formal-scoping}

\subsection{De Bruijn Indices}\label{sec:debruijn}

\begin{definition}[De Bruijn Index]\label{def:debruijn}
  A \emph{de Bruijn index} is a nameless representation of variables in
  which each variable is replaced by a natural number indicating the number
  of binders between the variable occurrence and its binding site.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[De Bruijn Indices]\label{ex:debruijn}
  \begin{align*}
    \lambda x.\, \lambda y.\, x & \quad\leadsto\quad \lambda.\, \lambda.\, 1 \\
    \lambda x.\, \lambda y.\, y & \quad\leadsto\quad \lambda.\, \lambda.\, 0 \\
    \lambda x.\, (\lambda y.\, x)\, x & \quad\leadsto\quad \lambda.\, (\lambda.\, 1)\, 0
  \end{align*}
\end{example}

De Bruijn indices eliminate the need for alpha-conversion: two
alpha-equivalent terms have identical de Bruijn representations.

\subsection{Named Environments vs. De Bruijn Levels}\label{sec:named-vs-debruijn}

\begin{definition}[De Bruijn Level]\label{def:debruijn-level}
  A \emph{de Bruijn level} counts from the bottom of the environment
  (the outermost binder) rather than from the top.  Unlike indices, levels
  are stable under the addition of new binders.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Named vs. De Bruijn Representation}
  \label{tab:debruijn-comparison}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Named} & \textbf{De Bruijn Index} & \textbf{De Bruijn Level} \\
    \hline
    $\lambda x.\, x$ & $\lambda.\, 0$ & $\lambda.\, 0$ \\
    $\lambda x.\, \lambda y.\, x$ & $\lambda.\, \lambda.\, 1$ & $\lambda.\, \lambda.\, 0$ \\
    $\lambda x.\, \lambda y.\, y$ & $\lambda.\, \lambda.\, 0$ & $\lambda.\, \lambda.\, 1$ \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Categorical Semantics of Scoping}\label{sec:categorical-scoping}

In categorical semantics, scoping is modeled using \emph{comma categories}
and \emph{pullbacks}.  The environment extension $\Gamma, x : A$ is a
morphism in the category of contexts, and variable lookup is a projection
morphism.

\begin{definition}[Context Extension]\label{def:context-extension}
  The context extension $\Gamma, x : A$ is the context obtained by adding
  the binding $x : A$ to $\Gamma$.  Categorically, it is the object part
  of a functor from the category of contexts to itself.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Nominal Techniques}\label{sec:nominal-techniques}

\begin{definition}[Nominal Logic]\label{def:nominal-logic}
  \emph{Nominal techniques} provide a framework for reasoning about
  binders and names that combines the advantages of named and nameless
  representations.  Names are treated as atoms with a permutation action,
  and binding is modeled using the \emph{freshness} relation
  $a \mathrel{\#} t$ (``atom $a$ is fresh for term $t$'').
\end{definition}

Nominal techniques (Gabbay and Pitts, 2002) provide:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Alpha-equivalence as a built-in notion (not a quotient).
  \item Induction and recursion principles that respect binding.
  \item A foundation for programming language implementations (the
    \texttt{nominal} package in Haskell, the \texttt{ocaml-nominal} library).
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 18.8 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:scoping-summary}

Scoping rules are among the most fundamental aspects of programming
language design.  Lexical scoping, the dominant paradigm, enables modular
reasoning and supports closures.  Dynamic scoping, while largely
superseded, remains useful for specific applications like configuration
and exception handling.

Closures are the runtime manifestation of lexical scoping: they capture
the environment at function creation time and use it at function
application time.  Their implementation---whether through static links,
heap-allocated environment records, or lambda lifting---is a central
concern of compiler design.

Hygienic macros extend the principles of lexical scoping to the level of
syntax manipulation, ensuring that macro expansion does not introduce
unintended variable captures.  This is essential for building reliable
abstraction mechanisms.

The formal semantics of scoping---through de Bruijn indices, nominal
techniques, and categorical models---provides the mathematical foundation
for understanding and implementing scoping correctly.
